ocrat running in a D+8 state during a national climate that polls show weighing on Fazio", "All eight expert raters call the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price Lamont near 97 percent", "Fazio outspent Lamont in September but has not narrowed the gap in any independent survey"], "rationale": "Every independent poll since summer shows Lamont ahead by double digits, raters unanimously place the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column, and prediction markets price the incumbent near 97 percent, all consistent with Connecticut's Democratic lean and Lamont's incumbency advantage.", "watch": "A standout debate performance from Fazio or a sharp late improvement in the national Republican environment could tighten the race, though closing a mid-teens gap would require an unusual shift.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ned Lamont (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Connecticut's governor's race features incumbent Democrat Ned Lamont seeking a third term against Republican Ryan Fazio. Lamont enters the final weeks with a clear advantage. Polls since September show him ahead by seven to twenty-three points, with most recent surveys in the mid-to-high teens, and aggregate estimates put his lead near fifteen to seventeen points. Polling also finds voters souring on the national economy and the Trump administration, an anchor on Fazio's campaign.\n\nFazio has stayed competitive on resources, outspending Lamont in September, and the two met in their first debate in late October. Still, no independent poll has shown him within single digits, and rating outlets uniformly place the race in the Solid or Safe Democratic column. Prediction markets give Lamont roughly a ninety-seven percent chance of winning another term.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.994, "margin": 15.28}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9913, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9896, "margin": 13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9893, "margin"